INT. TELEGRAPH OFFICE - LOCUST SEASON - DAY

Shuttered windows. A hum like a saw mill outside. Dust sifts through cracks. A single lamp.

MARSHAL EPHRAIM GREER, 40s, sweat-stained duster, wedges a rolled blanket under the door.

ODALYS, 30s, telegraph operator, taps a key. Nothing comes back.

Three drovers sit on crates. COAL BRANNIGAN, big, bearded. SILAS OAKLEY, old, hat in his lap. DESMOND YEBOAH, 20s, jittery, boots too clean.

Something skitters across the roof.

GREER
(to the room)
Nobody opens that door.

COAL
Wasn't planning to.

GREER
Keep the lamp low.

ODALYS
(to Greer)
Line's dead. Cut somewhere east.

GREER
Try Kearney.

ODALYS
I did.

GREER
Try again.

She does. The key clicks, hollow.

SILAS
(muttering)
Forty years. Never seen it this thick.

DESMOND
What'd he say?

COAL
He's talking to himself.

DESMOND
Is that a thing he does?

COAL
Since his wife passed.

Greer turns on them.

GREER
Quiet. All of you.

A thud on the door. Then another. Something heavier. Not an insect.

Everyone freezes. Odalys lifts her hand from the key.

GREER (CONT'D)
(low, to himself)
Not now.

He draws his revolver. Checks the cylinder.

COAL
Marshal.

GREER
Stay put.

COAL
You think it's him?

GREER
(to Coal)
I think it's someone who ought to be inside.

Desmond stands. Sits. Stands.

DESMOND
(to the room)
Do we open it or not?

SILAS
(muttering)
They'll eat the leather first.

The thud again. A voice, muffled, human, through the wood.

Odalys lunges for the lamp. Greer grabs the blanket from the door.

GREER
(to Coal, Silas, Desmond)
Back wall. Now.

They move. Guns come out. The doorknob begins to turn.
